That reasoning doesn't work for NetHack.

Now here is where a better gut level feeling for
statistics might help.

Derek, I think it was pointed out that while you
were running around burdened, you were as a result
spending more time on each level, time that the RNG
was seizing upon to spawn more monsters, that the
monsters were using to catch up with you, that
your nutrition was burning extra units, that you
were injuring your PC going down each stairwell,
that your pet was waking a scary sleeping monster,
usw.

It isn't the case that just because you cannot
immediately see the consequences of bad player
behavioral choices, those consequences somehow fail
to happen anyway.

Being habitually burdened is one quintessential such
bad behavioral choice. [A preference for melee
combat over ranged combat or over running away is
another.]

My personal favorite example of a "good behavioral
choice" that is less than convenient:

#untrap-ping a chest has a 50% chance of finding a
trap, per #untrap use.

Chests seem, mostly, to be generated untrapped
anyway.

Chest traps, mostly, don't kill the PC anyway,
directly.

         They tend to kill by indirect consequences
         like paralysis or blindness followed by some
         popcorn monster(s) eating your PC off at the
         ankles.

And yet, I do a #untrap at least 8 times on each
chest, reducing the fraction of times that I #loot a
chest with an unfound trap in it to somewhere way
below 1/256.

Why?

Because NetHack has so _many_ choices which can kill
your PC if you make those choices incorrectly, even
taking the 1/1000 chance of killing your PC per each
such choice pretty much guarantees your PC will fail
to ascend.

The lesson is, to win more often at NetHack, you
need to make "minimizing risk per move" a huge part
of your daily diet.

